# A relative path is accepted, not rejected, and the merged output carries
# its canonical absolute form — for MainSourceFile as well as for FilePath,
# since the two keys are the whole document.
#
# Issue #48 asked whether the merger should reject a relative FilePath
# outright, since a merged YAML containing one is only meaningful from the
# directory that produced it. Decision: fix it rather than reject it. The
# tool resolves the path against its own working directory and emits the
# absolute result, so the merged YAML is meaningful from any directory.

# RUN: rm -rf %t.dir
# RUN: mkdir -p %t.dir
# RUN: printf '%%80s' '' > %t.dir/foo.cpp
# RUN: printf '%%80s' '' > %t.dir/main.c

# RUN: cp %S/Inputs/single-replacement.tpl.yaml %t.dir/a.yaml
# (One -e per sed: under lit, BSD sed binds -i's suffix to the next word.)
# RUN: sed -i'' -e "s|MainSourceFile:  '@FOO@'|MainSourceFile:  'main.c'|; s|@FOO@|foo.cpp|g" %t.dir/a.yaml

# RUN: cd %t.dir && clang-ssaf-src-edit-merge -o %t.dir/merged.yaml %t.dir/a.yaml \
# RUN:     2> %t.dir/stderr.txt

# The input said 'main.c' and 'foo.cpp'; the output says where those are.
# Literals assume a symlink-free %t; see canonical-path-dedup.test.
# RUN: FileCheck --input-file=%t.dir/merged.yaml -DDIR=%/t.dir %s
# CHECK:      MainSourceFile:  '[[DIR]]/main.c'
# CHECK-NEXT: Replacements:
# CHECK-NEXT:   - FilePath:        '[[DIR]]/foo.cpp'
# CHECK-NEXT:     Offset:          10
# CHECK-NEXT:     Length:          3
# CHECK-NEXT:     ReplacementText: X
# CHECK-NEXT: ...

# Accepted silently: nothing on stderr at all. A cheap regression guard
# (a rejection would have failed the exit code above already); no weakening
# of the canonicalization makes it fire.
# RUN: FileCheck --input-file=%t.dir/stderr.txt --allow-empty --check-prefix=NOSTDERR %s
# NOSTDERR-NOT: {{.}}
